{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "types": ["vite/client"],

    "strict": true,
    /* The port from JavaScript was a rename, not a rewrite: the modules came
       across unchanged so that the frozen references still measure the same
       code. Two strictness flags are what that costs, rather than the whole of
       `strict`.

       This comment used to say that strictNullChecks, "which is where the real
       bugs are, stays on" — with the line below turning it off. Both halves
       had been here since the port. The comment is the half that was wrong,
       and it is worth being exact about which: the intent it describes is
       right and unmet, not a rule the code is breaking.

       Measured 2026-08-28, `tsc -p tsconfig.app.json --strictNullChecks`:
       **64 errors.** That is a session's work and not a sweep — each one is a
       judgement about whether a value can really arrive null, in code that
       writes the file a talker is built from — so it wants doing deliberately
       and in one piece, per CLAUDE.md §3. Note also that noImplicitAny hides
       some of the same ground: a value that is `any` is not null-checked
       either, so 64 is a floor. tsconfig.e2e.json turns both off too and has
       to move with this file. */
    "noImplicitAny": false,
    "strictNullChecks": false,

    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,

    "isolatedModules": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "composite": true,
    "tsBuildInfoFile": "./node_modules/.tmp/tsconfig.app.tsbuildinfo"
  },
  /* One program's worth of source, and that is what the split left.

     `loader` stood here too - the page that takes an exported file to a talker
     - along with `loader/tools/cable.js`, which needed allowJs above because
     it is JavaScript on purpose. adr/0012 took both to
     Lautstark/vorlaut-diy-talker, where the firmware they are held against
     already was. `third_party/` is deliberately absent and must stay absent:
     it holds a whole checkout of that repository for device/fixtures/, and a
     compiler that reads code out of it is the one edit that would quietly
     reinstate the dependency adr/0011 removed. */
  "include": ["src"]
}
